            140.57,
            317.13,
            153.86
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "Times-Roman"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            9.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "(2) The genus of a twist knot is 1 ([Ada94, p. 99]). Thus, the only torus knot which is",
          "bbox": [
            135.24,
            152.57,
            476.92,
            165.86
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "Times-Italic",
            "Times-Roman"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            9.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "a twist knot is the trefoil knot 31.",
          "bbox": [
            151.8,
            164.45,
            283.17,
            178.34
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "Times-Roman"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            6.97,
            9.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "(3) Twist knots are hyperbolic knots except the trefoil knot (see for example [Men84]).",
          "bbox": [
            135.24,
            176.45,
            477.09,
            189.74
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "Times-Italic",
            "Times-Roman"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            9.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "(4) It is well known (see for example [Rol90]) that the Alexander polynomial of the",
          "bbox": [
            135.24,
            188.33,
            476.98,
            201.62
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "Times-Italic",
            "Times-Roman"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            9.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "twist knot J(2, 2m) is given by ∆J(2,2m)(t) = mt2 + (1 −2m)t + m. Moreover, using",
          "bbox": [
            151.8,
            199.16,
            477.06,
            220.22
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "Times-Italic",
            "Times-Roman",
            "rtxmi",
            "rtxr",
            "txsy"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            6.97,
            9.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "the mirror image invariance of the Alexander polynomial, one has ∆J(2,2m+1)(t) =",
          "bbox": [
            151.8,
            212.33,
            476.98,
            226.22
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "Times-Italic",
            "Times-Roman",
            "rtxmi",
            "rtxr"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            6.97,
            9.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "∆J(2,−2m)(t). Thus the Alexander polynomial becomes monic if and only if m is ±1.",
          "bbox": [
            151.8,
            224.21,
            477.09,
            244.1
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "Times-Italic",
            "Times-Roman",
            "rtxmi",
            "rtxr",
            "txsy"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            6.97,
            9.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "As a consequence, the knot J(2, 2m) is not ﬁbered (since its Alexander polynomial",
          "bbox": [
            151.8,
            236.21,
            477.25,
            249.5
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "Times-Italic",
            "Times-Roman",
            "rtxmi"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            9.96
          ]
        },
        {
